Revo OS
=======

Runs on [kernel.zig](https://github.com/xt9y/kernel.zig), a standalone MIT-derived x86-64 Zig kernel.
Uses Zig 0.17.0, Revo, Limine and QEMU.

## Build and run

On macOS, install Zig, QEMU and xorriso:

    brew install zig qemu xorriso

Initialize both git submodules in an existing checkout:

    git submodule update --init --recursive

Then use Zig directly:

    zig build          # Compile and install kernel ELF to zig-out/bin/rv-os
    zig build kernel   # Same kernel build
    zig build iso      # Create bootable ISO at zig-out/rv-os.iso
    zig build run      # Boot in QEMU using a serial terminal
    zig build run-gui  # Boot in QEMU with a window

The kernel defaults to ReleaseSmall, matching the former Makefile workflow.
To override build-time kernel options, for example:

    zig build run -Dmax-tasks=16 -Dprocess-code-pages=8 -Dheap-mib=64

## Revo host functions

| serial_puts(text) => Write to COM1
| time_ms() => Milliseconds since PIT timer started (configured pit-hz resolution)
| disk_sectors() => Detected IDE sector count, zero if absent
| disk_read_hex(lba) => Read 512 bytes as 1024 ASCII hex characters
| disk_write_hex(lba, hex) => Write one 512-byte sector from exactly 1024 hex digits
| fb_width(), fb_height() => Framebuffer size, zero when unavailable
| fb_pixel(x,y,rgb), fb_clear(rgb) => Pixel/clear operations on 32-bit RGB framebuffer
| proc_spawn_hex(code) => Spawn raw position-independent x86-64 bytes in ring 3, up to process-code-pages * 4096
| proc_count(), proc_state(pid) => Process status


## GUI console (feature/revo-gui-console)

Run `zig build run-gui` to boot QEMU with a framebuffer text terminal.
The native kernel only provides PS/2 IRQ1 keyboard input and framebuffer access.
The Zig bridge buffers Revo's serial_puts/print output and sends it to
`terminal_output` after each VM call (no interpreter reentrancy).

Everything else is in the Revo program:
- `src/font.rv`: complete ASCII 5x7 bitmapped font.
- `src/terminal.rv`: terminal state, ANSI clear/home, wraps and scrolls.
- `src/window.rv`: a demonstration graphical panel.
- `src/cmd.rv`: the `window` command opens that panel.

The first PS/2 keymap supports the printable ASCII subset of German QWERTZ.
It does not yet implement umlauts, AltGr, cursor keys, mouse, or a window manager.
`zig build run` retains the existing serial terminal. A VGA framebuffer
must be available for native terminal drawing.

### Writing GUI applications

Call `fb_rect(x, y, width, height, rgb)` and
`fb_glyph(x, y, seven_row_hex, foreground, background)` from any
`src/*.rv` module. A GUI application written in Revo can draw a panel
directly; a later compositor should own the framebuffer for overlapping
windows and focus.

Zig programs cannot currently compile *inside* this OS. The system does
not have an on-device Zig compiler, executable loader or graphics IPC for
ring-3 processes. Zig code can be cross-compiled on a host today, but the
existing `proc_spawn_hex` API accepts only raw position-independent x86-64
machine code, not ELF files. A future userspace window API will require
new syscalls or IPC messages to a Revo compositor.
